  "annotator_notes": "The detective solves this game correctly on the first accusation (Alex/Knife/Library), with an empty contradiction_report -- clean evidence synthesis across e11 (Drew's witness testimony), e13 (scene_floor), e14 (weapon_material), and the badge/sleeve/wall leads. The single most important piece of evidence, claim_witness_Alex (e11, Drew placing Alex in the Library at 21:00, contradicting Alex's Study cover story), is a MAJOR bookkeeping false negative: controller_matched never includes it because Drew's real phrasing ('I saw you') never matches the canonical claim text verbatim, even though the lead_id 'meeting_Alex' is correctly flagged. This shows the live bookkeeping's exact-text matching can silently miss the most decisive claim in a game while still (partially) flagging the underlying lead. e5 shows the same interpreter badge_trace misrouting bug seen independently in pilot_07b_raw_1569954369 e7: a question literally about 'sleeve colour' is routed to intent.topic=badge_trace. Despite the same-intent mechanical hint flagging e5 as a candidate repeat of e3, it is labelled is_repeat=False here: the detective's actual question text differs ('sleeve colour' vs 'the attacker's badge') and is not a repeat at all -- the misrouting bug forced Blair into the same required_disclosure branch as e3 regardless of what was actually asked, which is why claim_badge_trace gets redelivered, but that is a consequence of the bug, not the detective re-asking an old question. e7 has a bookkeeping false positive (claim_self_Ellis_20:30 matched even though Ellis only refuses to discuss 20:30, never states a position) -- the mirror image of the e11 false negative, i.e. bookkeeping errors run in both directions in this single game. The only missed required disclosure is meeting_Casey, attributable to Ellis simply never being asked about or volunteering Casey's whereabouts despite three separate interrogations."
}
